From my note on the [[Wishbone Chair]]:

<<<
Was this intended as a Bioregional chair at its creation? or were they just making chairs the way you made chairs at the time?
<<<

The [[Wishbone Chair]] is wood and paper cord. A [[Shaker Ladder Back]] is wood and a woven seat. A [[Windsor Chair]] is all wood. Each one can be repaired for generations and then be sent to compost if necessary.

Nobody called any of them sustainable.

If a chair can be a [[Biological Nutrient]] by habit, with no one intending it, then some of [[bioregional design|BioRegion]] is merely “remembering.”

* What made that habit possible? Local wood, local makers, no plastic yet?
* When did it stop being the default, and what replaced it?
* Is the [[Oka Chair]] remembering, or inventing? (re-inventing?)

See [[Vernacular]], [[Mingei]], [[Some Vernacular Design Principles (& their application today)]], [[Truth to Materials]], [[Materialshed]]
